---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "manage",
 "name": "Azure DNS",
 "title": "How to Add and Manage DNS Records in Azure DNS",
 "description": "Add, edit, and delete DNS records in Azure DNS step by step, with the gotchas that break sites and mail, and free tools to verify every change.",
 "intro": "Managing DNS in Azure DNS is a short walk once you know where the records live and which defaults to watch. This is the complete flow, with the verification step most guides skip.",
 "panel": "Azure portal, search DNS zones, open the zone for your domain. Record sets are listed and created there.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open the DNS zone",
   "Portal, DNS zones, click the zone (create one first if missing, in a resource group)."
  ],
  [
   "Add a record set",
   "+ Record set: name, type, TTL, then value(s). Azure groups multiple values of one type into a single record set."
  ],
  [
   "Verify",
   "Record lookup on the name and type; Azure's anycast answers within its region network quickly."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Azure DNS zones bill per zone and per million queries, and the portal's record-set model (one set per name+type) confuses people expecting GoDaddy-style rows.",
  "fieldMap": { "name": "Name", "value": "Value", "ttl": "Default 3600 s", "apex": "@ (blank also works)", "note": "Records are grouped into record sets; editing a set edits every record in it." },
"faqs": [
  [
   "Azure DNS vs Azure Private DNS?",
   "Public zones answer for internet domains (this guide). Private zones resolve names inside virtual networks only."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Azure DNS?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Azure DNS's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for Azure DNS to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Azure DNS?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Azure DNS?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in Azure DNS, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
